.Ar PROG ARGS
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These are the procps applets of BusyBox 1.36.1 as built for Rach2.
Rach2 has no
.Pa /proc/PID/status
or
.Pa /proc/PID/maps .
.Bl -tag -width killall5
.It Nm free
Display free and used memory.
It reads
.Pa /proc/meminfo .
.It Nm fuser
Find processes using files or ports.
.It Nm iostat
Report CPU and I/O statistics.
Rach2 has no
.Pa /proc/diskstats ,
so the device report and
.Fl d
fail;
.Fl c
selects the CPU report alone.
.It Nm kill
Send a signal to the specified process IDs through
.Xr kill 2 .
The default signal is TERM.
.It Nm killall
Send a signal to the named processes through
.Xr kill 2 .
The default signal is TERM.
.It Nm killall5
Send a signal to all processes outside the current session, process 1 and
the processes of session zero excepted.
The default signal is TERM.
.It Nm lsof
Display all open files, its own excepted.
.It Nm mpstat
Report statistics for each processor.
.It Nm nmeter
Monitor the system in real time using
.Ar FORMAT_STRING .
.It Nm pgrep
Display processes selected by the regular expression
.Ar PATTERN .
.It Nm pidof
List the process IDs of all processes whose names match
.Ar NAME .
.It Nm pkill
Send a signal through
.Xr kill 2
to processes selected by the regular expression
.Ar PATTERN .
.It Nm pmap
Display process memory usage, from
.Pa /proc/PID/smaps
.Pq see Xr proc 5 .
The PSS column equals the resident size, since the kernel does not count
the processes that map a page.
.It Nm powertop
Analyze power consumption on Intel laptops.
Rach2 has none of the power-state and timer-statistics files it reads, so
it measures nothing.
.It Nm ps
List processes, reading
.Pa /proc/PID/stat
and
.Pa /proc/PID/cmdline .
.It Nm pstree
Display the process tree, optionally starting from
.Ar USER
or
.Ar PID .
.It Nm pwdx
Display the current directory of each specified process.
Rach2 has no
.Pa /proc/PID/cwd ,
so the applet reports
.Ql No such process
for every process.
.It Nm smemcap
Collect memory usage data from
.Pa /proc
and write it to standard output.
.It Nm sysctl
Display or set kernel parameters.
Rach2 has no
.Pa /proc/sys
for it to read.
.It Nm top
Display process activity, reading
.Pa /proc/PID/stat
and
.Pa /proc/PID/cmdline .
It reads the process status every
.Ar SECONDS
seconds and displays a screenful of processes.
.It Nm uptime
Display the time since the last boot.
.It Nm watch
Run
.Ar PROG
periodically.
.El
.Ss nmeter format
.Bl -tag -width "%[nINTERFACE]"
.It Xo
.Ql %Nc
.No or
.Ql %[cN]
.Xc
Display CPU use with a bar of size
.Ar N ,
default 10.
The letters S, U, N, D, I, and i denote system, user, niced, I/O wait,
interrupt, and software interrupt time, respectively.
.It Ql %[nINTERFACE]
Monitor the network interface
.Ar INTERFACE .
.It Ql %m
Allocated memory.
.It Ql %[md]
Dirty file-backed memory.
.It Ql %[mw]
Memory being written to storage.
.It Ql %[mf]
Free memory.
.It Ql %[mt]
Total memory.
.It Ql %s
Allocated swap.
.It Ql %f
Number of file descriptors in use, from
.Pa /proc/sys/fs/file-nr ,
which Rach2 does not have.
.It Ql %Ni
Total or specific interrupt rate.
.It Ql %x
Context switch rate, which Rach2 reports as a fixed zero.
.It Ql %p
Forks.
.It Ql %[pn]
Number of processes, from
.Pa /proc/loadavg ,
which Rach2 does not have.
.It Ql %b
Block I/O.
.It Ql %Nt
Time with
.Ar N
decimal places.
.It Ql %NT
Zero-based timestamp with
.Ar N
decimal places.
.It Ql %r
End lines with carriage return instead of line feed.
.El
.Ss top keys
.Bl -tag -width "N, M, P, T"
.It Ic N , M , P , T
Display CPU use and sort by process ID, memory, CPU use, or time,
respectively.
.It Ic S
Display memory; on Rach2 it ends with
.Ql no process info in /proc ,
since there is no
.Pa /proc/PID/smaps .
.It Ic R
Reverse the sort order.
.It Ic H
Toggle thread display; Rach2 has no
.Pa /proc/PID/task ,
and the scanner then skips every process.
.It Ic 1
Toggle the multiprocessor display.
.It Ic Q , No Ctrl-C
Exit.
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
.Ss fuser
.Bl -tag -width "-SIGNAL"
.It Fl m
Find processes using the same file system as the specified files.
.It Fl 4 , 6
Search only IPv4 or IPv6, respectively.
.It Fl s
Do not display process IDs.
.It Fl k
Kill the processes found.
.It Fl Ar SIGNAL
Send the specified signal; the default is KILL.
.El
.Ss iostat
.Bl -tag -width "-c"
.It Fl c
Display CPU utilization.
.It Fl d
Display device utilization.
.It Fl t
Print the current time.
.It Fl z
Omit devices with no activity.
.It Fl k , m
Use kilobytes or megabytes per second, respectively.
.El
.Ss kill, killall, killall5
.Bl -tag -width "-o PID"
.It Fl l
List all signal names and numbers.
.It Fl o Ar PID
For
.Nm killall5 ,
do not signal this process ID.
.El
.Ss mpstat
.Bl -tag -width "-I SUM|CPU|ALL|SCPU"
.It Fl A
Equivalent to
.Fl I Cm ALL Fl u Fl P Cm ALL .
.It Fl I Cm SUM|CPU|ALL|SCPU
Report interrupt statistics.
.It Fl P Ar num Ns No \&| Ns Cm ALL
Select the processor to monitor.
.It Fl u
Report CPU utilization.
.El
.Ss nmeter
.Bl -tag -width "-d MSEC"
.It Fl d Ar MSEC
Set the interval between updates in milliseconds.
The default is 1000; -1 specifies no delay.
.El
.Ss pgrep, pkill
.Bl -tag -width "-P PPID"
.It Fl f
Match the entire command line.
.It Fl n
Select only the newest process.
.It Fl o
Select only the oldest process.
.It Fl v
Negate the match.
.It Fl x
Match the whole name rather than a substring.
.It Fl s Ar SID
Match the session ID; 0 denotes the current session.
.It Fl P Ar PPID
Match the parent process ID.
.It Fl l
For
.Nm pgrep ,
display the command name too.
For
.Nm pkill ,
list all signals.
.It Fl a
For
.Nm pgrep ,
display the command line too.
.It Fl e
For
.Nm pkill ,
display the name and process ID of each process being killed.
.El
.Ss pidof
.Bl -tag -width "-o PID"
.It Fl s
Display only one process ID.
.It Fl o Ar PID
Omit the specified process ID.
Use
.Ql %PPID
to omit the parent of
.Nm pidof .
.El
.Ss pmap
.Bl -tag -width "-x"
.It Fl x
Display details.
.It Fl q
Use quiet output.
.El
.Ss ps
.Bl -tag -width "-o COL1,COL2=HEADER"
.It Fl o Ar COL1,COL2=HEADER
Select the columns to display.
.It Fl T
Display threads, which finds none on Rach2 (no
.Pa /proc/PID/task ) .
.El
.Ss pstree
.Bl -tag -width "-p"
.It Fl p
Display process IDs.
.El
.Ss sysctl
.Bl -tag -width "-e"
.It Fl e
Do not warn about unknown keys.
.It Fl n
Do not display key names.
.It Fl q
Use quiet output.
.It Fl a
Display all values.
.El
.Ss top
.Bl -tag -width "-d SEC"
.It Fl b
Use batch mode.
.It Fl n Ar N
Exit after
.Ar N
iterations.
.It Fl d Ar SEC
Set the delay between updates.
.It Fl m
Display memory, as with the memory display key, which on Rach2 ends with
.Ql no process info in /proc .
.It Fl H
Display threads, which finds none on Rach2.
.El
.Ss watch
.Bl -tag -width "-n SEC"
.It Fl n Ar SEC
Set the period in seconds, default 2.
.It Fl t
Do not print the header.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Dl free
.Dl pidof init
.Dl pidof /bin/sh
.Dl pidof /bin/sh -o %PPID
.Dl ps
.Dl uptime
.Dl watch date
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kill 2
.Sh HISTORY
